 **Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-* **Sparse Views:** The method relies on accurate camera poses, and performance may degrade with very sparse views or large viewpoint changes [S1].
-* **Local Minima:** Omitting novel view rays risks local minima, causing incorrect geometry [S1].
-* **Scale Mismatch:** Using a single depth prior ($L_d$) can lead to higher error later in training due to scale mismatches, whereas $L_{geo}$ refines depth progressively [S1].
+*   **Precision:** Reprojection errors are sensitive to camera pose accuracy. Inaccurate poses can lead to incorrect pseudo-ground truth selection [S1].
+*   **Boundaries:** The method assumes valid depth values from volume rendering. If a ray does not intersect any voxel, the depth may be undefined, requiring handling for missing data [G].
+*   **Degenerate Input:** In scenarios with significant viewpoint changes or sparse training views, the method may face challenges in generalization, as accurate camera poses are required [S1].
 
 ## Subtopics
 
-### Low-Resolution Guidance
-Uses low-resolution voxels as pseudo-ground truth early in training to guide coarse geometry learning, suppressing floaters effectively [S1].
+### Multi-scale Voxel Grids
+A hierarchical representation capturing varying scene frequencies, where low-resolution voxels guide coarse geometry and high-resolution voxels refine details. Chosen when few-shot inputs require robust geometry learning without overfitting. [Link](52-multi-scale-voxel-grids.md)
 
-### High-Resolution Refinement
-Medium- and high-resolution voxels gradually refine details as training advances, adapting to higher frequencies [S1].
-
-### Comparison with Depth Prior ($L_d$)
-$L_{geo}$ provides a stronger and more robust constraint than the depth prior, achieving lower final depth error and better convergence quality across scales [S1].
+### Reprojection Error Metric
+A measure of geometric consistency between views, used to select pseudo-ground truth depths. Chosen when explicit depth ground truth is unavailable. [Link](52-reprojection-error-metric.md)
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-* **Time Complexity:** Adds overhead for multi-scale rendering and reprojection calculations for each ray, but eliminates the need for complex scheduling or ground truth pose sampling [S1].
-* **Memory Complexity:** Requires storing multi-scale voxel representations and intermediate 3D points for reprojection [S1].
-* **Performance:** Significantly reduces floaters and improves convergence quality compared to training without geometric adaptation [S1]. Achieves lower final depth error than $L_d$ alone [S1].
-* **Strengths:** Self-adaptive, requires no external priors, and is robust to geometric noise compared to feature-warped methods like GeCoNeRF [S1].
-* **Limitations:** Requires accurate camera poses; generalization may suffer in scenarios with significant viewpoint changes or sparse training views [S1].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Depends on the number of scales $L$, the number of training rays, and the number of frame pairs used for reprojection. Each ray requires reprojection to $N-1$ other views and error calculation across $L$ scales.
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storing multi-scale voxel grids and RGB maps for all training frames.
+*   **Measured Performance:** In FrugalNeRF, Lgeo alone achieves lower final depth error than single-scale depth prior (Ld) and significantly reduces floaters [S1]. Combining Lgeo and Ld yields the best results [S1].
+*   **Strengths:** Self-adaptive, no complex scheduling required, robust to geometric noise compared to feature warping methods like GeCoNeRF [S1].
+*   **Limitations:** Relies on accurate camera poses; performance may degrade with sparse views or large viewpoint changes [S1].
 
 ## Background
-Cross-scale geometric adaptation was proposed in **FrugalNeRF** (2025) by the authors of [S1]. It was introduced to accelerate and regularize training for extreme few-shot novel view synthesis without learned priors [S1]. The method leverages weight-sharing voxels across scales to capture varying scene frequencies and uses reprojection errors to guide multi-scale geometry learning [S1]. It addresses the limitations of feature-based warping methods, which are highly sensitive to geometric noise and produce floaters [S1].
+Cross-scale geometric adaptation was proposed in FrugalNeRF (2025) by [S1]. It addresses the challenge of few-shot novel view synthesis by regularizing training through multi-scale voxel geometry and reprojection errors. It builds on the idea that low-frequency components can guide coarse geometry early in training, while high-frequency components refine details later.
 
